    "['clock source [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set system ntp source internal', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set system ntp source internal', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset system ntp source internal\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper doesn\\'t have an exact 1:1 equivalent for Cisco\\'s \"clock source internal\" command, as Juniper handles timing differently. The closest equivalent is to configure the system to use its internal clock as the NTP source. This assumes you\\'re setting up the device to act as an NTP server using its internal clock. \\n\\nFor precise timing applications (similar to PTP in Cisco), Juniper would use different PTP configuration commands under `set protocols ptp`.', 'trans_template': 'set system ntp source internal'}]]": 1,
    "['ptp clock domain [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp clock-mode boundary domain 255', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ptp clock-mode boundary domain 255', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the equivalent Juniper configuration command for `ptp clock domain 255`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ptp clock-mode boundary domain 255\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper command sets the PTP clock mode to \"boundary\" (matching the Cisco boundary mode) and specifies domain 255, which is the same as the Cisco configuration. Note that Juniper combines these settings into a single command where the clock mode and domain are configured together under the PTP protocol hierarchy.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp clock-mode boundary domain 255'}]]": 1,
    "['ptp clock priority [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ptp clock-option priority1 1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ptp clock-option priority1 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for `ptp clock priority 1`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols ptp clock-option priority1 1\\n##\\n\\nThis command sets the PTP clock priority1 value to 1 in Juniper's configuration, which is the equivalent parameter to Cisco's `ptp clock priority` command. Note that Juniper separates PTP priorities into priority1 and priority2 (where Cisco combines them in a single priority value), so we specifically set priority1 in this case.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ptp clock-option priority1 1'}]]": 1,
